    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of every layer and participates in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts*; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert.\n\nThese are orthogonal and frequently combined (e.g., TP within a node, EP across nodes), alongside data parallelism for request-level replication. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP inference: data flow, the collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer. The two main matmuls are sharded differently:\n\n**Attention and MLP \"column-parallel then row-parallel\" pattern:**\n\nFor a linear layer `Y = XW`:\n\n- **Column parallel**: `W` is split along its output dimension: `W = [W_1; W_2; ...; W_P]` across `P` ranks. Each rank computes `Y_i = X W_i`, producing a *sharded activation*. No communication needed before the matmul.\n- **Row parallel**: `W` is split along its input dimension: `W = [W_1, W_2, ..., W_P]`. Each rank holds `X_i` (sharded input) and computes a partial product `Z_i = X_i W_i`. The full output is the *sum* over ranks: `Z = \u03a3 Z_i`, which requires an **all-reduce**.\n\nA transformer feed",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
